    "research_classification": "OPEN-TRIAGE",
    "research_summary": "Appears to remain open. Whether a uniform deterministic bound on the independent stopping times forces the concatenation to be a two-sided Brownian motion is unresolved, and the source's remark that a finite-moment bound fails shows the threshold is delicate."
  },
  "complete_actual_prior_report": {
    "classification": "OPEN-TRIAGE",
    "wording_corrected": "no",
    "difficulty_suggested": null,
    "problem": "Statement is faithful to Problem 8 of Burdzy's \"My favorite open problems\" page. For each $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$, let $B^k$ be Brownian motion and $T_k$ a stopping time, with the stopped pieces independent, $0\\le T_k<\\infty$, and with their total lengths diverging in both time directions. Form a continuous process $X$ by concatenating the pieces $B^k_{[0,T_k)}$.\n\n**Question.** If there is a deterministic $c<\\infty$ such that $T_k<c$ almost surely for every $k$, must $X$ be a two-sided Brownian motion?\n\nThe worklist notes the source's remark that a uniform finite-moment bound on the stopping times is not sufficient.",
    "status_literature": "- Burdzy's author-maintained page still lists the item as open (accessed 2026-07-29); no solution notice is posted.\n- This is a \"concatenation of independent stopped Brownian paths with a common uniform bound on the stopping times\" characterization question. The obstruction (per the source) is that even a uniform finite-moment bound on the stopping times fails to force two-sided Brownian behavior.\n- No published resolution (a full characterization or counterexample settling the question) was located via web search through 2026.",
    "work_done": "- Verified the statement against the author's page and confirmed it is presented as open.\n- Searched for work on concatenations of stopped Brownian motions and characterizations of two-sided Brownian motion; found no resolution.",
    "result": "Appears to remain open. Whether a uniform deterministic bound on the independent stopping times forces the concatenation to be a two-sided Brownian motion is unresolved, and the source's remark that a finite-moment bound fails shows the threshold is delicate.",
    "what_remains": "- Determine whether the uniform bound $T_k<c$ a.s. (vs. only a moment bound) is the right sufficient condition.\n- A full characterization or a counterexample with bounded pieces that is not Brownian would settle the problem."
  },
